\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Interestingly, the method used to generate this graph is also a randomised algorithm -- the method of sampling. We used the idea of law of large numbers by saying that the number of failures divided by total number of runs (10000) should be a good approximation to the failure probability. Of course, 10000 was chosen because it was large as compared to the $n$ under consideration.}}{77}{figure.caption.64}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:randcheck}{{8.1}{77}{Interestingly, the method used to generate this graph is also a randomised algorithm -- the method of sampling. We used the idea of law of large numbers by saying that the number of failures divided by total number of runs (10000) should be a good approximation to the failure probability. Of course, 10000 was chosen because it was large as compared to the $n$ under consideration}{figure.caption.64}{}}
